  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nLow pressure is now well northeast of the area, over southern MN \nearly this morning. The associated stratus deck over northeast KS \nwill eventually clear from southwest to northeast across the area \ntoday as the system continues northward. CAA within northwest flow \ncontinues this morning until winds shift more westerly this \nafternoon. Temperatures in the 40s are expected to top out in the \nupper 50s today. Dew points are also lower than previous days, \nproviding more of a typical fall-like feel. \n\nA mid-level trough axis moves over the area tomorrow as another \nclosed low from the west phases with our departing system. A small \narea of light rain should stay south of the CWA tomorrow, but some \ncloud cover looks to move back in. Sfc and H5 ridging keep\nconditions dry for the end of the week. Temperatures will be slightly\ncooler in the mid 50s Friday afternoon before dipping into the mid \n30s by Saturday morning. A slight warming trend is expected late this\nweekend and early next week with a ridge building aloft. Long range \nmodels show another low pressure system becoming cutoff late this \nweekend, moving somewhere into the central CONUS. Exact locations \ndiffer but most solutions keep weather dry across the region into the\nmiddle of next week.",
